WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.040
The

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:40.760
Natural History Museum. One of the most popular of all London's attractions. Sometimes he gets

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:46.640
so crowded that it can be quite difficult to see the exhibits as closely as you might wish.

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:50.440
Ladies and gentlemen, the museum is going to be closing in five minutes,

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:58.120
so please make your way towards the exits. Thank you. So it's a great treat if somehow or other

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:26.440
you can manage to look around when all the other visitors have gone.

00:01:58.120 --> 00:02:24.280
Some of the creatures here you might, if you were lucky, have seen in the wild.

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:34.120
But there are certain ancient animals that we'll never see with our own eyes.

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:40.120
Because they're extinct.

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:51.840
And among them are one or two mysterious, not to say suspicious characters that I

00:02:51.840 --> 00:03:12.160
would like to examine as they were when they were alive. It's a big place. There are 70

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:18.680
million or so specimens here, I'm told. And the first I want to look at right now is way

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:29.440
up on the very top floor. This, some might say, is the most scientifically important

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:36.360
and valuable specimen in the whole of the museum. It's a fossil called Archaeopteryx,

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:41.920
and it was secured from the museum by the first director, Professor Richard Owen,

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:48.040
back in 1862. Getting it wasn't easy. There was a lot of international competition,

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:54.880
and there was a certain amount of skullduggery, and it certainly cost a small fortune. But

00:03:54.880 --> 00:04:10.240
what kind of creature was Archaeopteryx when it was alive? It had two long leg bones, so it must

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:22.160
have stood upright. A bony tail and a long neck. Its head had bony jaws packed with teeth like

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:31.080
a reptile's, and its arms had three elongated fingers, each ending with a claw. So you might

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:40.040
think it was some kind of strange, spindly-armed, upright-standing lizard. Except for one thing,

00:04:40.040 --> 00:05:03.840
one fact. There is evidence of more than just bones on its slab. Feathers. Archaeopteryx

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:10.640
lived some 150 million years ago, long before the appearance of true birds. Those feathers

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:33.820
on its arms certainly enabled it to glide. But that's not all. It had powered flight.

00:05:33.820 --> 00:05:42.400
Marks on the bones show that there were enough muscles attached to them to enable it to flap.

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:48.360
Not only that, a recent scan of its skull showed that its brain would have given it

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:55.000
the senses and reactions that are needed for accurate control in the air. This creature

00:05:55.000 --> 00:06:01.440
was half-reptile, half-bird. It was the first proof that in prehistory, there were intermediate

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:19.520
forms that link the big, very different groups of animals that we know today. But while Archaeopteryx

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:24.720
could certainly fly, it could also clamber up tree trunks and along the branches like

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:35.200
a tree-living reptile, thanks to those clawed fingers. There were insects flying around

00:06:35.200 --> 00:07:03.080
at that time, and Archaeopteryx's teeth show that it was a hunter. And this is Professor

00:07:03.080 --> 00:07:10.520
Richard Owen, the man who acquired that fossil and built this museum. Although he disagreed

00:07:10.520 --> 00:07:15.800
with Darwin's views on evolution, he was one of the great scientists of his time, and he

00:07:15.800 --> 00:07:26.040
had a particular flair for interpreting fossils. In 1839, a huge thigh bone was sent to the

00:07:26.040 --> 00:07:32.320
museum from New Zealand. Owen deduced from its internal structure that it must have belonged

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:40.640
to a bird. If so, it must have been a giant. The maoris of New Zealand had stories of giant

00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:45.440
flightless birds that had once roamed their islands, but Europeans had dismissed them

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:53.320
as myths. But eventually, Professor Owen acquired enough bones of these huge birds to put together

00:07:53.320 --> 00:08:10.840
a complete skeleton of one of them. This was no myth. The maoris in their legend had called

00:08:10.840 --> 00:08:19.080
it a moa, and Professor Owen in his researches had proved that it once had existed. But was

00:08:19.080 --> 00:08:26.080
it the largest bird that had ever lived?

00:08:49.080 --> 00:09:06.080
There were several different species of moa, but this one was the biggest. It stands three

00:09:06.080 --> 00:09:19.000
meters tall. But is this really what it looked like when it was alive? You can tell how unusual

00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:24.400
an animal holds its head from the junction between the skull and its neck. If that is

00:09:24.400 --> 00:09:30.640
underneath the skull, then its neck would have been upright. But this moa's neck joint

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:45.640
is at the back of the skull, so it must have held its neck more horizontally. Like this.

00:09:45.640 --> 00:10:13.640
So, was the giant moa the biggest bird that has ever existed? Well, if it craned up its

00:10:13.640 --> 00:10:36.320
neck, it was almost certainly the tallest. You might think that such a gigantic bird

00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:50.560
would have no enemies in the remote and isolated forests of New Zealand. But you would be wrong.

00:10:50.560 --> 00:11:03.360
Tonight, strange things are happening in the Natural History Museum. The giant moa that

00:11:03.360 --> 00:11:09.520
was wandering around New Zealand until only a few centuries ago was so huge, it's difficult

00:11:09.520 --> 00:11:17.120
to believe that it had any enemies, any predators. Well, there's also a Maori legend of a huge

00:11:17.120 --> 00:11:23.120
predatory bird, an eagle, that existed at the same time. And what is more, there are

00:11:23.120 --> 00:11:42.240
bones to prove it. This colossal bird was nearly twice as heavy as today's most powerful

00:11:42.240 --> 00:11:49.320
eagle. Bringing down a giant moa must have been a huge task. They too were strong and

00:11:49.320 --> 00:12:14.080
heavy. But the eagle had powerful eyesight. A beak the size of a butcher's cleaver. And

00:12:14.080 --> 00:12:20.680
razor sharp talons as big as the claws of a tiger. The Greek for grappling hooks is

00:12:20.680 --> 00:12:44.040
harpax. And that word gives this bird its name. This is harpagonis. It was a deadly

00:12:44.040 --> 00:13:03.440
predator. It was the largest eagle that has ever existed. And it lived in the same forests

00:13:03.440 --> 00:13:24.360
as the moas. We know that harpagonis preyed on moas because moa skeletons have been found

00:13:24.360 --> 00:13:33.560
with holes stabbed through their pelvic bones that exactly match the grasp of the eagle's

00:13:33.560 --> 00:13:39.200
claws. It was probably even strong enough to cling to a moa's back with one foot while

00:13:39.200 --> 00:13:52.280
it slashed at its victim's neck with the other. But it looks as if this moa is going to escape

00:13:52.280 --> 00:14:13.400
for now. As well as its millions of specimens of animals and plants, the museum also has

00:14:13.400 --> 00:14:19.160
huge and fascinating archives, scientific journals from all over the world, letters

00:14:19.160 --> 00:14:27.800
from explorers, even posters and handbills if they have anything to do with natural history.

00:14:27.800 --> 00:14:33.320
In the 19th century when Professor Owen was in charge of this museum, new and extraordinary

00:14:33.320 --> 00:14:38.800
things were turning up from all over the world. And Professor Owen was very keen that his

00:14:38.800 --> 00:14:44.800
museum should have the best of them. He secured the archaeopteryx from Germany, the moas from

00:14:44.800 --> 00:14:51.520
New Zealand, but sometimes really strange things turned up on his very doorstep. And

00:14:51.520 --> 00:14:57.080
there were certainly lots of very odd creatures being exhibited around London in Victorian

00:14:57.080 --> 00:15:05.160
times. This print shows an extraordinary monster that was being displayed in Piccadilly. An

00:15:05.160 --> 00:15:10.840
American showman called Albert Koch was charging a shilling ahead to have a look at it. Professor

00:15:10.840 --> 00:15:17.800
Owen decided to investigate. He felt sure that something was wrong with it, but nonetheless

00:15:17.800 --> 00:15:24.800
he was intrigued. And he bought it. When he'd got it back to his museum, he was able to

00:15:24.800 --> 00:15:45.040
examine it in detail. It was certainly gigantic and bigger than anything else he had in his

00:15:45.040 --> 00:15:51.200
museum at the time. Koch, the showman, had dug up the bones from a farmer's field in

00:15:51.200 --> 00:15:57.520
Missouri and maintained that in life the animal had stood nine metres long and almost five

00:15:57.520 --> 00:16:04.680
metres tall. There were claims that this was a fearsome predator that used its extraordinary

00:16:04.680 --> 00:16:12.400
tusks for stabbing its victims, presumably by swinging its head sideways. Well, I'm

00:16:12.400 --> 00:16:18.880
sure Professor Owen would have had something to say about that. He must have realised that

00:16:18.880 --> 00:16:26.160
these blunt, rounded ridges on these huge molar teeth would be very effective at grinding

00:16:26.160 --> 00:16:33.680
up twigs and fur cones and rough forest vegetation, but they lack the sharp blade that you need

00:16:33.680 --> 00:16:41.680
to slice through flesh. This is not the jaw of a carnivore. It soon became clear that

00:16:41.680 --> 00:16:47.920
Koch had increased the size of his monster skeleton by adding extra vertebrae, ribs and

00:16:47.920 --> 00:16:58.720
even blocks of wood. The Missouri Leviathan was a fraud. So Owen removed all the extra

00:16:58.720 --> 00:17:17.640
bits. And then he put the real bones back together in their true form. Finally, he detached

00:17:17.640 --> 00:17:26.120
those astonishing tusks and put them back in the correct way. It seems obvious now,

00:17:26.120 --> 00:17:43.360
but in life they had pointed in much the same direction as those of a modern elephant.

00:17:43.360 --> 00:17:52.120
So here today stands not Koch's Leviathan, but Owen's Mastodon, a vegetarian relative

00:17:52.120 --> 00:17:58.320
of the elephant that lived 12,000 years ago in North and Central America. It may have

00:17:58.320 --> 00:18:18.720
decreased a bit in size, but it's still an astonishing animal. Our understanding of the

00:18:18.720 --> 00:18:26.240
Mastodon is a lot more accurate today, thanks to Professor Owen. But it was not the only

00:18:26.240 --> 00:18:36.480
creature in his museum to be the victim of misrepresentation. This poor old bird is a

00:18:36.480 --> 00:18:44.200
dodo. It once lived on the island of Mauritius in the Indian Ocean, and it's almost certainly

00:18:44.200 --> 00:18:51.720
the first animal species that human beings actually exterminated in historic times. And

00:18:51.720 --> 00:19:04.000
so now we talk about being as dead as a dodo. But in spite of its fame, this one is a fake.

00:19:04.000 --> 00:19:12.920
Its feathers come from a goose, its feet are muddled on a turkey, and its beak, I suspect,

00:19:12.920 --> 00:19:21.400
is plaster. The museum can be forgiven because no skin or feathers of the dodo survive. Its

00:19:21.400 --> 00:19:27.360
image was influenced by pictures like this one, painted by a 17th century Dutch artist,

00:19:27.360 --> 00:19:35.880
Roland Savory. But he had never seen a living dodo and based his image on accounts by seafarers.

00:19:35.880 --> 00:19:41.680
I've often wondered whether dodos actually look like that. But unfortunately, they'd

00:19:41.680 --> 00:19:59.560
all disappeared before anyone could get a good look at them. Until now. This funny,

00:19:59.560 --> 00:20:08.680
dumpy creature is how the bird is usually represented these days. But I've seen quite

00:20:08.680 --> 00:20:17.600
a lot of flightless birds over the years, and this one doesn't quite ring true. An

00:20:17.600 --> 00:20:24.080
examination of the way its thighs join its pelvis has shown that in life, it actually

00:20:24.080 --> 00:20:36.600
stood much more upright. We now know that its feathers were probably a lot fluffier

00:20:36.600 --> 00:20:43.360
than in that painting. We also now know that it was related to the pigeon, and some experts

00:20:43.360 --> 00:20:55.400
suggest that it made a pigeon-like call, doo-doo, doo-doo, which gave the bird its name. The

00:20:55.400 --> 00:21:04.120
dodo probably felt on fruit. There was a lot of it on the island. I'll try them with

00:21:04.120 --> 00:21:15.400
a stick. What do you make of that? Oh! That's a very powerful beak. In fact, it may well

00:21:15.400 --> 00:21:21.800
have been adapted for crushing shells and crustaceans for the sake of the calcium.

00:21:21.800 --> 00:21:29.200
And there's a female. Maybe she is another reason why they had such large beaks to show

00:21:29.200 --> 00:21:50.400
off with during courtship. And here comes a rival male. He could be another reason for

00:21:50.400 --> 00:22:17.080
having a huge beak to fight with in disputes over nest sites. Until now, no one has ever

00:22:17.080 --> 00:22:44.880
seen a dodo egg, so no one knows how big it was. After tonight, who knows? Science has

00:22:44.880 --> 00:22:51.840
revealed the truth behind many a myth and discovered some creatures that are so odd

00:22:51.840 --> 00:23:04.040
as to be scarcely believable. But there is one story that is still remarkably persistent.

00:23:04.040 --> 00:23:10.280
Back in 1951, a famous Himalayan explorer and mountaineer, Eric Shipton, came across

00:23:10.280 --> 00:23:15.640
some footprints across a high snow field that looked as if they'd been made by some kind

00:23:15.640 --> 00:23:26.920
of giant ape. Shipton's Sherpa companions had no doubt about what had made them. A yeti.

00:23:26.920 --> 00:23:37.600
An abominable snowman. Well, there is one small, insignificant-looking specimen in the

00:23:37.600 --> 00:23:47.720
storage vaults down here that could perhaps explain those prints. It was found in a shop

00:23:47.720 --> 00:24:04.120
in Hong Kong that sold Chinese traditional medicines. It was a molar tooth of some kind

00:24:04.120 --> 00:24:11.760
of ape-like creature, except that it was huge. The museum has only got a fragment. This is

00:24:11.760 --> 00:24:20.280
it. But here's a cast of a complete one, and it's six times the size of one of ours. It

00:24:20.280 --> 00:24:27.760
was given the name Gigantopithecus, a giant ape. After that discovery, one or two more

00:24:27.760 --> 00:24:33.560
teeth were discovered, but nothing much until eventually a piece of the lower jaw was found.

00:24:33.560 --> 00:24:41.080
The original is now in America. This is a cast. But here is the lower jaw. If this animal

00:24:41.080 --> 00:24:46.960
had a skull with the same proportions as those of a gorilla, its complete skull would have

00:24:46.960 --> 00:25:05.360
been this big. This was a true monster.

00:25:16.960 --> 00:25:46.760
So we know a huge ape did exist, Gigantopithecus. It could well have stood in the middle of

00:25:46.760 --> 00:25:52.160
the road, three meters tall, in which case it would have been eight times as heavy as

00:25:52.160 --> 00:25:58.240
I am. And if you're as heavy as that, you don't spend much time climbing in trees, because

00:25:58.240 --> 00:26:05.480
they won't support you. So the likelihood is that his arms are quite short, and he walked

00:26:05.480 --> 00:26:08.480
upright. He was bipedal.

00:26:08.480 --> 00:26:18.160
Get out of the way.

00:26:38.480 --> 00:26:45.800
An upright animal has its head on the top of its spine, as I do. And if that head is

00:26:45.800 --> 00:26:52.720
to be well balanced, it's better not to have a long muzzle, but a rather flat face. So

00:26:52.720 --> 00:27:00.240
if I were to observe Gigantopithecus and it stared back at me, I suspect I'd find its

00:27:00.240 --> 00:27:24.840
look rather unnervingly familiar.

00:27:24.840 --> 00:27:30.720
Gigantopithecus is commonly thought to have died out several hundred thousand years ago,

00:27:30.720 --> 00:27:36.920
but sightings of the yeti continue to be reported. So is it possible that some kind of giant

00:27:36.920 --> 00:27:44.120
ape, maybe even Gigantopithecus itself, still survives somewhere out in those remote Himalayan

00:27:44.120 --> 00:27:57.840
mountains?

00:27:57.840 --> 00:28:06.560
The Gigantopithecus tooth isn't the only intriguing specimen down here in the storerooms.

00:28:06.560 --> 00:28:13.240
This is a piece of dung. Looking at it, you might think it had dropped to the ground only

00:28:13.240 --> 00:28:26.040
yesterday. It was found in a cave in Patagonia. And with it, a piece of skin like this, covered

00:28:26.040 --> 00:28:35.600
in a very coarse bristly hair, and on the underside, mysterious white bone nodules,

00:28:35.600 --> 00:28:44.200
as though it was a kind of armour. No known creature alive today has armoured hide like

00:28:44.200 --> 00:28:51.640
this. If it still survived, it would be a truly extraordinary discovery. So at the end

00:28:51.640 --> 00:29:02.600
of the 19th century, explorers and scientists started a search for it. In fact, the dung

00:29:02.600 --> 00:29:07.840
and the fur appeared to be recent only because there'd been in effect freeze dried in that

00:29:07.840 --> 00:29:17.360
ancient cave. The creatures themselves had died out some 10,000 years ago. But explorers

00:29:17.360 --> 00:29:25.560
did find their skeletons. They were giant sloths that lived not in trees as modern ones

00:29:25.560 --> 00:29:46.920
do but on the ground. And this one had immense claws. What could it have used them for?

00:29:46.920 --> 00:30:14.800
These ground sloths probably spent most of their time on all fours. But nonetheless,

00:30:14.800 --> 00:30:21.800
they were perfectly capable of rearing up on their hind legs. And when they did that,

00:30:21.800 --> 00:30:26.880
they probably stood about three metres tall, which is as tall as a grizzly bear, if not

00:30:26.880 --> 00:30:45.080
taller. But I don't think this one is going to use its claws on me. That dung made it

00:30:45.080 --> 00:30:50.020
clear that these creatures are vegetarians, so they doubtless use those claws for ripping

00:30:50.020 --> 00:31:06.120
up plants. But it's been discovered recently that they use them for something else as well.

00:31:06.120 --> 00:31:34.680
Something that seems rather surprising for animals of their great bulk. They dug burrows.

00:31:34.680 --> 00:32:01.040
Huge excavations like this have been found all over Patagonia. And we know they were

00:32:01.040 --> 00:32:06.080
made by giant sloths because scratches on the walls of the burrows exactly match their

00:32:06.080 --> 00:32:16.640
claws. Such immense burrows must have been excellent places to take refuge. And the giant

00:32:16.640 --> 00:32:22.080
sloths may well have had need of them because there was a truly ferocious predator living

00:32:22.080 --> 00:32:42.360
alongside them. A great cat with immense sabre-shaped teeth. Smiled on. For me, there is no more

00:32:42.360 --> 00:32:52.240
alarming animal in the whole museum than this. And its skeleton is perfectly preserved because

00:32:52.240 --> 00:32:58.880
about 10,000 years ago it wandered into a pool of naturally occurring tar oozing from

00:32:58.880 --> 00:33:06.560
the ground in California. In general shape, it was somewhat like a lion, but more muscular

00:33:06.560 --> 00:33:13.760
and much heavier. And those sabre teeth were really sharp. No wonder the giant sloths needed

00:33:36.560 --> 00:33:43.400
You might think that Smiledon would have caught its prey as a lion often does by chasing it,

00:33:43.400 --> 00:33:53.920
leaping on it at speed and then throttling it, suffocating it with a bite to the neck.

00:33:53.920 --> 00:34:11.800
But Smiledon stalked its prey, creeping quietly across the plains until it got really close.

00:34:11.800 --> 00:34:31.560
And then it pounced. Smiledon couldn't throttle its prey with those huge teeth and they were

00:34:31.560 --> 00:34:41.440
too brittle to slash. They would shatter if they struck bone. Instead, the animal would

00:34:41.440 --> 00:34:49.800
have first used its great weight to pin down its victim. Then it would have used its sabres

00:34:49.800 --> 00:34:58.460
like blades to slice open the soft flesh of its victim's throat. But these terrifying

00:34:58.460 --> 00:35:05.520
hunters had a rather touching side to their characters. Tigers today are solitary hunters

00:35:05.520 --> 00:35:12.440
and when one gets too old to hunt successfully, it dies. But skeletons of really elderly sabre

00:35:12.440 --> 00:35:20.160
tooths have been discovered, which suggests that not only did Smiledon hunt in packs,

00:35:20.160 --> 00:35:24.680
but when members of the family were too old to hunt for themselves, they were allowed

00:35:24.680 --> 00:35:49.720
to take a share of the kill. The museum is full of creatures that appear terrifying,

00:35:49.720 --> 00:35:54.360
but which no doubt, if you knew them better, would prove to have quite a charming side

00:35:54.360 --> 00:36:11.920
to their characters. But there is one here that would, I think, chill everyone's blood.

00:36:11.920 --> 00:36:17.900
London's Natural History Museum is one of the world's greatest treasuries. It holds

00:36:17.900 --> 00:36:34.580
some 70 million specimens, some beautiful, some bizarre and some truly frightening. This

